          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "433093",
          "text": "<p>Consider the function:</p><pre data-pbcklang=\"python\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\" class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-python\">def foo(X=\"a\", Y=[]):\n    X = X + X\n    Y.append(X)\n    print(X, Y)\n</pre><p><br>When called in a loop:</p><pre data-pbcklang=\"python\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\" class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-python\">for i in range(4): foo()</pre><p><br>Output is:</p><pre data-pbcklang=\"python\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\" class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-python\">aa ['aa']\naa ['aa', 'aa']\naa ['aa', 'aa', 'aa']\naa ['aa', 'aa', 'aa', 'aa']\n</pre><p><br>Why is the list ' $Y$ ' growing with each call?<br> </p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>The string ' $X$ ' is immutable, but the list ' $Y$ ' is mutable and retains its state between function calls.</li><li>Both ' $X$ ' and ' $Y$ ` are mutable, causing the function to append values repeatedly.</li><li>The list ' $Y$ ' is reset to an empty list with each function call, causing the unexpected output.</li><li>The function is using recursion, which causes the repeated output.</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "a",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-python-2,goclasses,python-programming,programming,two-marks",
          "category": "Programming in Python"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "433104",
          "text": "<p>Consider the following Python code:<br> </p><pre data-pbcklang=\"python\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\" class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-python\">number = 8\n\n\ndef adder(integer):\n    print(number)\n    number = 10\n    print(number + integer)\nadder(5)\n</pre><p><br>What will be the output of the code when the function `adder(5)` is called?<br> </p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>8&nbsp;&nbsp; 15</li><li>8 &nbsp;&nbsp;13</li><li>UnboundLocalError: local variable 'number' referenced before assignment</li><li>10 15</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-python-2,goclasses,python-programming,programming,one-mark",
          "category": "Programming in Python"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "433092",
          "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;color:#000000;background-color:transparent;font-weight:400;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;white-space:pre;white-space:pre-wrap;\" id=\"docs-internal-guid-3b624bf7-7fff-0ddb-cef9-99a5f52dd85f\">Consider the following Python code:</span></p><pre data-pbcklang=\"python\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\" class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-python\">my_list = []\nmy_list += \"Python\"\nprint(my_list)\n\n<span style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Arial,sans-serif;color:#000000;background-color:transparent;font-weight:400;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;white-space:pre;white-space:pre-wrap;\" id=\"docs-internal-guid-3b5bf96b-7fff-5809-57d9-d5231f39b7c2\">What will be the output of running this code?</span>\n</pre><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li><pre data-pbcklang=\"python\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\" class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-python\">['Python']</pre></li><li><pre data-pbcklang=\"python\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\" class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-python\">['P', 'y', 't', 'h', 'o', 'n']</pre></li><li><pre data-pbcklang=\"python\" data-pbcktabsize=\"4\" class=\"prettyprint linenums lang-python\">['P', 'y', 't', 'h', 'o', 'n', 'Python']</pre></li><li>&nbsp;An error will ocuur.</li></ol>",
